@page "/demo"

<PageTitle>Mercury Outpost · Demo</PageTitle>

<section class="demo">
    <h1 class="demo-title">Mercury Outpost</h1>

    <div class="demo-body">
        <figure class="planet">
            <img class="planet-image" src="images/mercury.jpg" alt="The planet Mercury, photographed by the MESSENGER spacecraft" />
            <figcaption class="planet-credit">NASA / JHUAPL / Carnegie Institution of Washington</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="folds">
            <details class="fold">
                <summary>Outpost Robot Rules</summary>
                <ol class="rules-list">
                    <li>A robot may not injure a human being or, through inaction, allow a human being to come to harm.</li>
                    <li>A robot must obey the orders given it by human beings except where such orders would conflict with the First Law.</li>
                    <li>A robot must protect its own existence as long as such protection does not conflict with the First or Second Law.</li>
                </ol>
            </details>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="folds stories">
        <details class="fold">
            <summary>Runaround</summary>
            <div class="story">
                <p class="story-credit">Story by Isaac Asimov (1942).</p>
                <p>
                    Two engineers reopen an abandoned mining station on the sunlit side of Mercury, bringing with them a new and very expensive robot.
                    They soon find that the station's banks of photo-cells, the only thing standing between them and the Sun's heat, are failing.
                    Repairing them takes selenium, and the nearest supply is a pool of it several miles away across open, scorching ground.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Only the robot can make the trip, so they send it to bring selenium back.
                    They give the order casually, the way one asks for any routine errand.
                    Hours pass, and the robot does not return.
                </p>
                <p>
                    When they finally find it, it has not reached the pool.
                    It is circling it, round and round at the same distance, staggering as it goes and answering their calls with nonsense.
                    The heat is rising, and the engineers are running out of time.
                </p>
            </div>
        </details>
    </div>
</section>
